.TH APLAY 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
aplay \- play WAV files on a sound card
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aplay
.RB [ \-D
.IR device ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aplay
plays each WAV
.I file
on a sound card, one after another.
With no
.IR file ,
or where a
.I file
is
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input.
.PP
A file's header says its format, and
.B aplay
sets the card to it: PCM (unsigned 8-bit or signed 16-bit) or IMA ADPCM, mono
or stereo, at 8000, 11025, 22050 or 44100 samples a second.
It writes the file's data as it reads it (a write waits while the card's buffer
is full), then waits for what it queued to play out before the next file.
.PP
A stereo sound comes from two speakers either side of the block, its left
channel on your left as you face its front; a mono one from the block.
Any other music can be converted on another computer, for example with
.BR ffmpeg :
.PP
.nf
ffmpeg \-i song.mp3 \-ar 22050 \-ac 2 \-acodec adpcm_ima_wav song.wav
.fi
.PP
A file that cannot be opened, read or played is reported on standard error, and
.B aplay
goes on with the next one.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-D " device"
The sound card's file (\fB/dev/dsp\fR, the first card's, unless given).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if every file was played; 1 if any could not be, or the card could not be
opened.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
aplay song.wav
aplay \-D /dev/dsp1 alarm.wav
.fi
.SH NOTES
ALSA's
.B aplay
has many more options (raw formats, recording with
.BR arecord ,
devices by name); mcos's takes WAV files only.
A raw file plays as it is with
.BR "cat file > /dev/dsp" ,
at OSS's defaults: 8000 samples a second, unsigned 8-bit, mono.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cat (1)
